{"id":"bc-144","statement":"`count` stays alive after `makeCounter` returns.","truth":true,"explanation":"The returned function closes over the binding, so the variable outlives the call that created it. This is how module-style privacy works without a class.","level":"intermediate","category":"javascript","subject":"closures","codeSnippet":"function makeCounter() {\n  let count = 0;\n  return () => ++count;\n}","codeLanguage":"javascript","codeHtml":"<span style=\"color:#FF79C6\">function</span><span style=\"color:#50FA7B\"> makeCounter</span><span style=\"color:#F8F8F2\">() {</span><br><span style=\"color:#FF79C6\">  let</span><span style=\"color:#F8F8F2\"> count </span><span style=\"color:#FF79C6\">=</span><span style=\"color:#BD93F9\"> 0</span><span style=\"color:#F8F8F2\">;</span><br><span style=\"color:#FF79C6\">  return</span><span style=\"color:#F8F8F2\"> () </span><span style=\"color:#FF79C6\">=></span><span style=\"color:#FF79C6\"> ++</span><span style=\"color:#F8F8F2\">count;</span><br><span style=\"color:#F8F8F2\">}</span>"}